{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JR27MSbY4YTDofGK2GkBbn","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bs6KYBAUrpBc5ZoAUCxqoL","session_id":"3cb85483-5d7e-436c-98a4-1967ad6de50f","uuid":"4906233e-efb9-4bb1-9000-be66d96037b7","timestamp":"2026-05-20T08:43:47.656Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"afa8567a5433ff140","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgPyJFYQT28Pr8DJWcSprj","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":52970,"tool_uses":6,"duration_ms":136261},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"02c8b531-315e-4e88-ae01-63e21e3a0851","session_id":"3cb85483-5d7e-436c-98a4-1967ad6de50f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0185PutpHJnnhLwimji6PL3N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018SCqcaUaorJQ2qsPtPu7SF","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Review\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how multi-class (K>=3) non-differential annotation errors interact with DAG topology in causal inference, breaking the classical toward-null guarantee that holds for binary misclassification. The authors derive closed-form bias expressions across six canonical DAG topologies, introduce Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs) analogous to E-values, and build a correction guidance framework. The work is validated through extensive Monte Carlo simulations and empirical data from three LLMs on three CSS tasks (~270K annotations). The paper is technically ambitious, well-structured, and addresses a genuine gap in the LLM-annotation pipeline for causal studies.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n### 1. Technical Soundness: 5 (Accept)\n\n**Closed-form bias derivations.** The K=3 closed-form bias expressions across six topologies are correctly motivated. The derivation strategy — expressing observed conditional distributions in terms of true distributions and C, then computing distortion in the estimand — is standard and appropriate. The 97.2% theory-MC match rate (35/36 combinations within 95% MC CIs) provides strong validation. The single boundary case (front-door + rank-deficient C, discrepancy 0.003, resolving at N=50,000) is handled transparently.\n\n**Matrix-form generalization.** The extension to general K via matrix-form expressions with three interpretability tools (eigendecomposition, worst-case bound, spectral diagnostics) is well-conceived. The top-2 eigenvalues capturing 87% of bias variance is a useful simplification, and the 88% tightness of the spectral norm bound is honestly reported with the caveat about block-diagonal structures.\n\n**ASV formal proofs.** Theorems 1 (Monotonicity) and 2 (Sharpness) are stated for the correct scope (confounding, mediation, collider). The proof techniques are appropriate: algebraic for confounding (spectral gap), envelope theorem for mediation (product-of-coefficients), Lagrangian/KKT for collider. The constructive nature of the sharpness proofs (explicit C*) adds both theoretical and practical value. However, the paper provides only proof sketches in the main text — full proofs presumably appear in a supplementary that is not included. This is a significant gap for verification.\n\n**Remaining topologies.** The honest treatment of M-bias, instrument, and front-door ASV bounds as conjectures (with numerical sharpness gaps < 0.008) is appropriate given the algebraic complexity. Zero monotonicity violations across 1000 scenarios is reassuring.\n\n**Concern:** The non-differential misclassification assumption is load-bearing throughout. The paper acknowledges differential misclassification as future work (Section 9) but does not discuss how violations of this assumption in practice (which are likely with LLMs, since error rates may depend on context) would affect the taxonomy. This is an unstated fragility.\n\n### 2. Experimental Design & Execution: 5 (Accept)\n\n**MC simulation design.** The 6-topology x 6-C-structure x 1000-rep design at N=10,000 is well-powered. The six confusion matrix structures (diagonal-dominant, moderate off-diagonal, uniform off-diagonal, block-diagonal, near-permutation, rank-deficient) cover the relevant space systematically. The 100K random C search for direction reversal detection is thorough.\n\n**K=5,7 scaling.** The scaling experiments on confounding + mediation + collider are well-designed and produce the expected monotonic increase in violation rates. Theory-MC match degradation at K=7 (16/18) is correctly attributed to increased MC variance and resolved at N=50,000.\n\n**Empirical validation.** 3 LLMs x 3 CSS tasks x ~30K annotations x 3 prompt variants (~270K total) is a substantial empirical effort. The task selection (protest K=5, stance K=3, toxicity K=2) provides diversity in K and difficulty. The K=2 toxicity task as a consistency check (all biases toward null) is a smart design choice.\n\n**Binarization collapse.** The 7-topology x 3-K analysis is complete and the finding that 12% of K=5,7 scenarios exhibit direction reversal from binarization alone is a strong result that preempts a natural reviewer objection.\n\n**Correction framework.** The 432-scenario MC validation (6 topologies x 6 C structures x 3 K values x 4 effect sizes) is systematic. The 95% accuracy (411/432) is credible, and the failure mode analysis (14 MLA-PPI ties, 7 extreme-C instabilities) is transparent.\n\n**Coverage vs. YAML reference:** All 10 planned experiments (plan_001 through plan_010) are implemented and reported. The numbers in the paper match the expected results from the design reference with high fidelity. No planned experiment is missing or incomplete.\n\n### 3. Reproducibility: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n**MC simulations.** The paper specifies N=10,000 (and N=50,000 for boundary cases), 1000 replications, and the six C structure types, which is sufficient for reproducing the theoretical validation. However, the exact parameterizations of the six C structures (e.g., what \"diagonal-dominant\" means quantitatively — what diagonal values?) are not given in the main text.\n\n**Empirical setup.** The three LLMs and three tasks are named, and ~30K per model-task is stated. However, prompt templates are not provided, and the three \"prompt variants\" that cause C structure shifts (Frobenius distance 0.04-0.12) are not described. Dataset sources are not specified — which protest event coding dataset? Which stance detection dataset? These are critical for reproduction.\n\n**annot-sensitivity toolkit.** The claim of a pip-installable package with >90% test coverage, K=2-20 support, and <1s runtime for K<=7 is plausible given the mathematical nature of the computations. Six Jupyter notebook tutorials and minimal dependencies (NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib) suggest a serious software effort. However, no URL or repository link is provided (expected given blind review), so the claim cannot be verified.\n\n**Missing details:** (a) How gold-standard labels were obtained for the empirical confusion matrices; (b) exact structural parameters in the MC simulations; (c) how the \"topological context\" was assigned for ASV calibration (Table 12) — i.e., how do you decide that protest coding should be evaluated in a \"mediation\" context vs. a \"collider\" context?\n\n### 4. Statistical Rigor: 5 (Accept)\n\n**Coverage calibration.** Table 6 reports ASV coverage at 90%, 95%, 99% nominal levels across three topologies. The actual coverages (91.2%, 95.2%, 98.8% on average) show slight conservative over-coverage, which is expected and benign. The per-topology breakdown is informative — mediation (94.9%) and collider (94.8%) are slightly under the nominal 95% but within acceptable bounds.\n\n**Correction framework accuracy.** The 95% claim (411/432) is well-supported by the topology-level breakdown (Table 9), which shows front-door at 90.3% as the weakest link. The decomposition of the 21 failures into 14 MLA-PPI ties and 7 genuinely unstable cases is rigorous.\n\n**Spectral gap correlation.** R^2 = 0.83 for spectral gap predicting bias magnitude (Table 14) is reported alongside the correlation coefficient (r = -0.91) and the catastrophic threshold (< 0.15). The joint model (spectral gap + diagonal dominance, R^2 = 0.89) is a useful addition. These appear trustworthy given the systematic MC exploration behind them.\n\n**Held-out coverage.** The 94% held-out coverage (vs. 95% nominal) on empirical data is honestly reported and the slight under-coverage is correctly attributed to sampling variance in C estimation from ~30K annotations.\n\n**Minor concern:** The ASV concordance metric (8/9 scenarios, Table 12) is a rank-order comparison across only 3 models. With 3 items, there are only 6 possible orderings, and matching 8/9 could partially reflect chance. A statistical test for rank concordance (e.g., Kendall's tau with a p-value) would strengthen this claim.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment (vs. YAML Reference)\n\n| Plan | Description | Status |\n|------|-------------|--------|\n| plan_001 | Multi-topology bias derivation + MC (K=3) | **Fully covered** — Tables 1, 2; all numbers match |\n| plan_002 | General K expressions + K=5,7 scaling | **Fully covered** — Tables 3, 4 |\n| plan_003 | Empirical confusion matrices (3 LLM x 3 task) | **Fully covered** — Tables 10, 11 |\n| plan_004 | ASV formal proofs (3 priority topologies) | **Fully covered** — Theorems 1-2, Table 6 |\n| plan_005 | ASV MC validation (remaining 3 topologies) | **Fully covered** — Table 7, Conjecture 1 |\n| plan_006 | ASV calibration on empirical data | **Fully covered** — Tables 12, 13 |\n| plan_007 | Correction guidance framework | **Fully covered** — Tables 8, 9 |\n| plan_008 | Binarization collapse analysis | **Fully covered** — Table 5 |\n| plan_009 | Pathological C analysis + structural diagnostics | **Fully covered** — Table 14 |\n| plan_010 | annot-sensitivity toolkit | **Covered** (described in Section 8, no verifiable artifact) |\n\nAll 10 planned experiments are reported. The figures described in the YAML (Figs 1-20) are not all present in the paper — the PDF contains only tables, no figures. This is a notable omission from the experiment design.\n\n---\n\n## Top 3 Strengths\n\n1. **Topology-dependent dichotomy is the right framing.** The clean separation into information-loss topologies (confounding, instrument, M-bias) vs. structural-distortion topologies (exposure, mediation, collider, front-door) is both theoretically elegant and practically actionable. This directly challenges the widespread \"non-differential = conservative\" assumption and does so with a complete taxonomy rather than isolated counterexamples.\n\n2. **The ASV framework fills a genuine methodological gap.** The analogy to E-values is apt, and the constructive sharpness proofs (explicit C* achieving the bound) go beyond typical sensitivity analysis by giving practitioners an interpretable \"nearest dangerous confusion matrix.\" The coverage calibration is thorough and the results are well-calibrated.\n\n3. **Comprehensive experimental coverage with honest scoping.** All planned experiments are executed, the binarization collapse analysis preempts a key objection, the 15% \"no adequate method\" region is honestly identified rather than swept under the rug, and sharpness conjectures are clearly distinguished from proofs. This level of intellectual honesty strengthens credibility.\n\n## Top 3 Weaknesses\n\n1. **No figures in the paper.** The experiment design reference describes 20+ figures (heatmaps, violin plots, scatter plots, sensitivity curves, PCA projections, decision trees). The submitted PDF contains zero figures — only tables. This is a major presentation gap. The core finding (topology-dependent reversal) would be far more compelling with the planned heatmap (Fig 1 in YAML) showing reversal zones. Without visual evidence, the reader must reconstruct spatial intuitions from tabular data alone. For a paper with a visual density target appropriate for a top venue, this is a serious deficiency.\n\n2. **Non-differential assumption is fragile and under-discussed.** The entire framework assumes P(V-tilde | V, other variables) = P(V-tilde | V). With LLMs, this is unlikely to hold strictly — error rates depend on text difficulty, domain, and context. The paper mentions differential misclassification only as future work in the conclusion. A section discussing (a) how robust the results are to mild violations, (b) what signature differential errors would leave in the confusion matrix, and (c) whether the empirical data show evidence of differentiality would significantly strengthen the paper.\n\n3. **Supplementary materials with proofs are missing.** Theorems 1 and 2 are core contributions, but only proof sketches (one paragraph each) appear in the main text. For a paper whose theoretical contribution rests on these proofs, the absence of a supplementary appendix with full derivations is a significant gap. Reviewers cannot verify correctness of the envelope theorem argument for mediation ASV or the KKT conditions for collider ASV from the sketches alone.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On non-differentiality:** Have you tested whether the empirical confusion matrices from plan_003 satisfy the non-differential assumption? Specifically, do error rates vary by, e.g., text length, topic, or co-occurring variables? If so, how would the bias taxonomy change?\n\n2. **On topological context assignment:** In Table 12, each task is evaluated in multiple \"topological contexts\" (e.g., protest in mediation, collider, instrument). How were these contexts chosen? Are they based on actual downstream causal analyses, or hypothetical placements? If hypothetical, the empirical validation is weaker than presented.\n\n3. **On the correction framework's \"no adequate method\" zone:** The 15% of scenarios where no method works involve correlated multi-LLM errors. Given that Kim et al. (2025) showed 60% same-direction errors, and your own data confirm 58%, this seems like a large practical gap. Do you have any preliminary results on dependence-aware correction, or is this purely aspirational?\n\n4. **On figures:** Were figures omitted due to page constraints, or are they not yet generated? The paper is currently 10 pages of main text + 1 page of references, within EMNLP's 8+unlimited references limit if the main text is compressed. But the absence of any visual representation of the core findings is striking.\n\n5. **On the toolkit:** Can you confirm the toolkit exists and is functional? Section 8 describes it but provides no evidence (no code snippets, no example output, no benchmark results). Will it be available for artifact evaluation?\n\n---\n\n## Overall Recommendation: **4.0 — Weak Accept / Conference**\n\n**Justification:** The paper makes a genuine theoretical contribution (topology-dependent bias taxonomy for multi-class misclassification), the ASV framework is well-motivated and carefully validated, and the experimental coverage is comprehensive. The 432-scenario MC validation and ~270K empirical annotations represent substantial effort. The core finding — that the same confusion matrix can be benign in one DAG topology and catastrophic in another — is important for the growing community using LLM annotations in causal studies.\n\nHowever, three issues prevent a stronger recommendation: (1) the complete absence of figures undermines presentation quality and makes key results harder to parse; (2) the non-differential assumption deserves more scrutiny given the LLM context; and (3) the missing proof appendix makes it impossible to fully verify the theoretical claims (Theorems 1-2). If the authors address these in revision — particularly adding the planned figures and the proof appendix — this paper would merit a score of 4.5-5.0. The intellectual content is at the conference level; the presentation is not yet there.\n","project":"causal_annot_error_attrib","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260520T074549","overall_score":"4.0 — Weak Accept / Conference"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":729,"cache_read_input_tokens":52212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":729,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgPyJFYQT28Pr8DJWcSprj","session_id":"3cb85483-5d7e-436c-98a4-1967ad6de50f","uuid":"2121f4cd-647c-41b2-9002-2aae93d7756a"}
